---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Opposing Data Stories | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Same data, opposite stories: chart one dataset two ways using design choices alone, then reveal them side by side."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Opposing Data Stories
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Nov 21 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CST
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Same data. Opposite stories.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Join tcplot for a morning of honest lies: we'll chart one
                dataset two ways, once as a crisis and once as nothing to see,
                without changing a single number.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white">
                        <img
                            src="/images/zollner-illusion-1860.svg"
                            alt="The Zöllner illusion: long parallel lines crossed with short hatching that makes them appear to tilt"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        The Zöllner illusion (1860): every long line in this
                        figure is perfectly parallel. Your eye reads every
                        chart before you do.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act one: bring a chart that spins
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
                Your ticket in is one chart and two minutes on the story it
                tells: a chart that misled you, a chart that changed your mind,
                or a chart where you can't tell which one it did.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act two: spin it both ways
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                In pairs, take one dataset from our local starter set and two
                briefs: make it look alarming, then make it look like nothing.
                One rule: the data stays the same. Every row, no fake numbers,
                no cherry-picking. Only design choices are allowed:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-600 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
                <li>axis ranges, baselines, and aspect ratio</li>
                <li>binning, chart type, and scale</li>
                <li>titles, annotations, and emphasis</li>
                <li>the illusions your eye can't refuse</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
                Then the reveal: each pair puts their two charts up side by
                side, and the room guesses which choice is doing the work.
                We'll name every trick, and close with what an honest version
                looks like.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No coding needed. Datawrapper, a spreadsheet, or pen and paper
                all work, and coders are welcome to bring D3, Observable Plot,
                matplotlib, or ggplot2. Bring a chart with a story and, if you
                want to build digitally, a laptop.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                >.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
